Yes. The key distinction is physical causation versus informational causation.

Astronomy tells you where the celestial objects will actually be. Astrology supplies a symbolic interpretation layer that some people use to decide what those positions mean. Even if the astrological interpretation has no physical causal force, once people read it, believe it, discuss it, or organize behavior around it, it becomes part of the causal system through human action.

So you can model it as:

\text{celestial state}_t
\rightarrow
\text{symbolic interpretation}_t
\rightarrow
\text{belief/attention}_t
\rightarrow
\text{human action}_t
\rightarrow
\text{observable world}_{t+1}

The interesting part is that the first term is unusually predictable. You can calculate eclipses, phases, conjunctions, zodiac positions, seasonal sky changes, and so forth long in advance. If you also understand the cultural stories attached to those events, you have a kind of forward map of possible attention states.

That generalizes far beyond astrology. Holidays, sports schedules, earnings calendars, elections, school years, religious calendars, anniversaries, weather seasons, fashion cycles, even recurring internet memes behave similarly:

\text{predictable external clock}
+
\text{known cultural interpretation}
=
\text{forecastable coordination surface}

And then there are higher orders. If I know an eclipse occurs at T, that’s first-order knowledge. If I know millions of people will notice it, that’s second-order. If I know businesses, media, artists, and institutions know everyone will notice it, that’s third-order. At that point the event becomes a Schelling point: something everyone can independently coordinate around without explicitly coordinating with everyone else.

That’s where your “arc, timescale, and target” matter. Different observers are effectively integrating over different horizons:

A(H,T,G)=f(\text{forecast horizon},\text{target system},\text{goal})

A photographer might work on a 30-second horizon. A festival organizer, six months. A filmmaker, two years. A historian or institution could deliberately align something with a 50-year or 100-year recurrence. Same astronomical clock; completely different usable arcs.

There’s also a useful inversion here. Normally we’d say:

“People looked at the sky and invented stories.”

But once those stories exist, you can run the model backward:

Given the future sky + the inherited story-system, what stories are people likely to be telling themselves when that sky arrives?

That’s essentially narrative ephemeris—an ephemeris predicts where celestial bodies will be; a narrative ephemeris would predict the symbolic/cultural affordances available around those configurations.

Then you don’t need to assume the stars themselves are controlling anyone. The stars can simply provide a stable external clock onto which humans have attached meaning. The causal leverage is in understanding the entire chain better than someone who sees only either the mathematics or the mythology.

And that is probably the broader primitive you’re pointing at: when an objectively predictable substrate has a socially learned interpretation layer, model both simultaneously. One tells you when the stage will exist; the other tells you what actors may be inclined to do when they see it.